    keyRule: "Minnesota has a genuine four-tier burglary system, the most granular surveyed among all states so far. First degree covers entering without consent with criminal intent when the building is an occupied dwelling, or the offender is armed or has an explosive, or an assault occurs, carrying a mandatory minimum 6 months for the occupied-dwelling variant. Second degree covers unauthorized entry with intent involving a dwelling, a bank or pharmacy entered by force, possession of burglary tools, or entering a government, religious, historic, or school building intending theft or fraud. Third degree covers unauthorized entry intending to steal or commit a felony or gross misdemeanor, or repeatedly entering a public building to steal after having a prior theft-related conviction. Fourth degree, the lowest tier, covers unauthorized entry intending only a non-theft misdemeanor, or repeat unauthorized entry into a public building.",
