    keyRule: "Similar to the pattern found for the District of Columbia and New Jersey earlier in this project, Arizona's domestic violence statute is purely a definitional and procedural designation, not a standalone crime: it defines which relationships qualify (current/former spouses or household members, parents of a shared child, blood or legal relatives, current/former romantic or sexual partners) and requires the underlying offense's charging documents to carry a 'DV' designation, but the offense itself is prosecuted and classified entirely under whichever general statute applies (most often assault). It does add real procedural consequences: mandatory arrest when the incident involves physical injury or a weapon, temporary firearm seizure authority, mandatory victim notification about protective orders, and up to a 2-year sentencing increase if the defendant knew the victim was pregnant.",
