    citation: "S.C. Code Ann. § 16-25-20 (domestic violence)",
    keyRule: "A genuine, dedicated domestic violence statute with a real three-degree structure (unlike DC, New Jersey, Arizona, Pennsylvania, Maryland, Wisconsin, and Colorado's confirmed absences earlier in this project, though matching the genuine standalone statutes found for Minnesota and, earlier, Tennessee). The base offense is causing physical harm or injury to a household member, or attempting/threatening to with apparent ability and circumstances creating fear of imminent peril. Third degree (no aggravating factors) is a misdemeanor, $1,000-$2,500 fine or up to 90 days. Second degree (moderate injury, one prior conviction within 10 years, a protective-order violation during a third-degree offense, or specified aggravating circumstances like a minor present, pregnancy, or breathing impairment) is a misdemeanor, $2,500-$5,000 or up to 3 years. First degree (great bodily injury, two or more priors within 10 years, a firearm, or a protective-order violation during a second-degree offense, among other factors) is a felony, up to 10 years.",
    penaltyClass: "Third degree: misdemeanor, $1,000-$2,500 or up to 90 days; second degree: misdemeanor, $2,500-$5,000 or up to 3 years; first degree: felony, up to 10 years",
