    keyRule: "Nevada does not use the word 'rape'; sexual assault covers non-consensual sexual penetration, forcing the victim to penetrate themselves or another, or acting under conditions where the perpetrator knows or should know the victim cannot resist or understand the conduct, plus a separate, more severe path for sexual penetration of a child under 14. It is a Category A felony throughout. Standard sexual assault carries life with parole eligibility after 10 years (or life without parole if substantial bodily harm resulted); against a victim under 16, that floor rises to 25 years (or life without parole with substantial bodily harm); against a victim under 14, the floor is 35 years. A prior conviction for child sexual assault or an equivalent offense mandates life without parole regardless of the current victim's age. A minor defendant no more than 2 years older than the victim, absent force or incapacity, faces reduced charges.",
