    keyRule: "A direct read of Iowa's first degree murder statute (§707.2) confirmed felony murder is tied to the defined statutory term 'forcible felony' (§702.11), the same distinctive structure this project's own prior session already identified for Iowa. The statute's other paths include willful/deliberate/premeditated killing, killing while escaping lawful custody, intentionally killing specific protected persons (peace/correctional officers, public employees, hostages) while imprisoned, killing a child during child endangerment or assault under circumstances manifesting extreme indifference to human life, and killing during an act of terrorism. Murder in the first degree is a class 'A' felony. No third degree classification exists; Iowa's citation database has entries only for first and second degree murder, consistent with this direct confirmation.",
